#!/usr/bin/env -S python3 -u
"""
arcs-serve: the same page, in the habitat where you can act on it.

The work-arcs page has been a published artifact for months and that was right: an
artifact is reachable from a phone, survives this laptop, and costs nothing to hand to
somebody. What it cannot do is touch this machine. Every card on it names a session, a
worktree, a branch that exists twenty centimetres away, and the only thing the page can
offer about any of them is text you re-type into a terminal. The verdict on that was
that one brain is in orch and one is in the arcs page and the seam between them is a
human being copying a uuid.

This is the other habitat. Not another page -- the SAME page, the same bytes arcs-refresh
already wrote to ~/.local/state/work-arcs/page.html, served at 127.0.0.1 where a click
can reach tmux. The published artifact stays what it is: read, acknowledge, file. Here,
and only here, the verbs exist.

Two habitats, one page
----------------------
The rule this daemon exists to keep is that there is never a second page to maintain. The
artifact and the cockpit render from one build; what differs between them is capability,
never content, and never the clock. A card that says nothing useful on the artifact would
say nothing useful here either, and a fact discovered here has to reach the artifact by
being in the page, not by being in this process.

That is why this file serves a file and does not render one. It has no opinion about
arcs. It reads page.html off the disk and hands it over, and the entire reason it is a
daemon rather than `python3 -m http.server` is the three paragraphs below.

Charset, which is not a nicety
------------------------------
The stored page has no <meta charset>. It never did: what arcs-page emits is a fragment
that the artifact runtime wraps in its own <head>, and that head is where the encoding
declaration lives. Served naked over HTTP with no charset in the Content-Type, a browser
falls back to its locale default and every em-dash, arrow and ✓ on the page becomes
mojibake -- which is exactly what happened the first time this page was opened from
file://. So this daemon states utf-8 on the wire. It is the one thing it must not
delegate.

no-store for the same family of reason: the page is rebuilt every morning under the same
path, and a cockpit showing yesterday's arcs because a validator matched is a cockpit
that lies quietly. There is nothing expensive here to cache -- it is a local file read.

The verbs, and why they are tmux and not orch
---------------------------------------------
`open` and `opendir` are 1:1 bridges to tmux and nothing else. The first draft of this
was going to shell out to `orch resume` and `orch watch`, and a survey of orch's
internals found those are vestigial or gated on $TMUX being set -- a daemon started by
systemd has no $TMUX, so `orch watch` would have refused every request. What orch itself
actually does, in term_host.py, is the invocation reproduced here verbatim: sessions live
as DETACHED tmux sessions named by their bare uuid on the `orch-sessions` socket, and you
reach one by attaching to it. So that is what this does. The seam for the tests is
therefore $WORK_ARCS_TMUX_BIN and not an orch path: stubbing a binary this program never
calls would test nothing.

The daemon adds no judgement of its own on top of either verb. It validates the target,
runs the argv list, and relays what tmux said verbatim -- including the refusals. It will
not start a tmux server: if the default server or its `orch` session is absent, that
absence IS the answer, returned as the error, because a daemon that quietly spawns a
window host is a daemon that puts your windows somewhere you did not ask for.

Security, which is the whole reason this is not a convenience
-------------------------------------------------------------
This process turns an HTTP request into a command on Kyle's machine. Three properties
hold that safe and none of them are optional:

  bind 127.0.0.1 ONLY. Not 0.0.0.0 with a firewall in front, not "localhost" resolved by
  whatever the resolver feels like. The listening socket's address is asserted in the
  tests by reading /proc/net/tcp, because a comment claiming loopback is not loopback.

  POST /act requires the header X-Arcs-Act: 1, and 403s without it. This is the CSRF
  guard and it works by arithmetic rather than by trust: a page on any other origin
  cannot set a custom request header without a CORS preflight, and this daemon never
  emits an Access-Control-* header of any kind, so the preflight can never succeed. A
  hostile page in another tab can therefore make this daemon 403, and nothing else. The
  same guard is on /term for the same reason -- it starts a process, so it is an act.

  Nothing else is served. Not a directory listing, not a sibling of page.html, not a
  path traversal dressed up in %2e%2e. Four routes exist and everything else is 404.

Targets are validated before they are arguments: a uuid must match the uuid shape, a
directory must expanduser+realpath to an existing directory under $HOME. Both verbs run
as argv lists -- there is no shell string anywhere in this file for a target to be
interpolated into.

What GET means here
-------------------
/ and /health are side-effect-free, always, and /health in particular is a read a page
may poll: it probes tmux with a 2s timeout and caches the answer for thirty seconds, so
polling it costs one pair of subprocesses a half-minute rather than one pair a second.

/term/<uuid> is the exception and is documented as one. It starts a ttyd if there is not
already one for that uuid, which is a side effect; it is idempotent per uuid, reaped when
the ttyd exits, capped at four concurrent, bound to lo, and behind the same X-Arcs-Act
header as the verbs. It answers 200 with JSON rather than the 302 that would be the
obvious thing, because the fetch that had to carry the CSRF header cannot read a
Location off an opaque redirect -- and what the terminal drawer needs is the URL, not a
navigation.

Configuration
-------------
  WORK_ARCS_SERVE_PORT       what to listen on                        (default 7379)
  WORK_ARCS_PAGE             the page to serve      (default $STATE/page.html)
  WORK_ARCS_SERVE_LOG_LINES  ring-trim arcs-serve.log to this many    (default 2000)
  WORK_ARCS_TMUX_BIN         the tmux to shell out to                 (default tmux)
  WORK_ARCS_TTYD_BIN         the ttyd to spawn for /term              (default ttyd)
  WORK_ARCS_TERM_MAX         concurrent ttyds                         (default 4)

A systemd --user unit ships in systemd/work-arcs-serve.service. It is deliberately not
enabled by anything in this repo; see the comment at its head.
"""

import argparse
import json
import os
import re
import signal
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
import threading
import time
from datetime import datetime
from http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, ThreadingHTTPServer
from pathlib import Path
from urllib.parse import unquote, urlparse

STATE = Path(os.environ.get("XDG_STATE_HOME") or (Path.home() / ".local" / "state")) / "work-arcs"
PAGE = Path(os.environ.get("WORK_ARCS_PAGE") or (STATE / "page.html"))
LOG_FILE = STATE / "arcs-serve.log"
LOG_LINES = int(os.environ.get("WORK_ARCS_SERVE_LOG_LINES") or 2000)
TMUX = os.environ.get("WORK_ARCS_TMUX_BIN") or "tmux"
TTYD = os.environ.get("WORK_ARCS_TTYD_BIN") or "ttyd"
TERM_MAX = int(os.environ.get("WORK_ARCS_TERM_MAX") or 4)

# The window host. Both verbs put a window in this session on the default server, and
# neither will create it -- see the module docstring.
ORCH_SESSION = "orch"
ORCH_SOCKET = "orch-sessions"

# A uuid and nothing that merely contains one. Anchored both ends: `..;rm -rf` with a
# uuid in the middle is not a session id.
UUID_RE = re.compile(r"\A[0-9a-fA-F]{8}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}"
                     r"-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{12}\Z")

ACT_HEADER = "X-Arcs-Act"
ACT_TIMEOUT = 15      # seconds, per tmux invocation
PROBE_TIMEOUT = 2     # seconds, for the health probes
HEALTH_TTL = 30       # seconds the health answer is reused for


# --- saying what happened ---------------------------------------------------------------

_log_lock = threading.Lock()


def log(msg):
    """Append one line, and never let the log be the thing that kills the serve loop."""
    line = "%s  %s\n" % (datetime.now().astimezone().strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S%z"), msg)
    try:
        with _log_lock:
            LOG_FILE.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
            with LOG_FILE.open("a", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                fh.write(line)
    except OSError:
        pass
    if sys.stderr.isatty():
        try:
            sys.stderr.write(line)
        except OSError:
            pass


def trim_log():
    """Ring-trim, the same way arcs-refresh does: a daemon nobody watches still has to
    be readable a month later, and an unbounded log is a log you stop opening."""
    try:
        if not LOG_FILE.exists():
            return
        with _log_lock:
            lines = LOG_FILE.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").splitlines(True)
            if len(lines) <= LOG_LINES:
                return
            LOG_FILE.write_text("".join(lines[-LOG_LINES:]), encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError:
        pass


# --- running things ---------------------------------------------------------------------

def child_env():
    """$TMUX unset, exactly as orch's own term_host does before it touches tmux. An
    explicit -t target makes this mostly moot, but a daemon inheriting a pane's $TMUX
    from whatever shell started it is the kind of ambient state that makes a bug depend
    on how you launched the program."""
    env = os.environ.copy()
    env.pop("TMUX", None)
    env.pop("TMUX_PANE", None)
    return env


def run(argv, timeout=ACT_TIMEOUT):
    """(returncode, combined output). Never raises: a missing binary and a hung tmux are
    both ordinary answers here, and both have to reach the caller as text it can relay."""
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=timeout, env=child_env())
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return 127, "%s: not found" % argv[0]
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return 124, "%s: no answer in %ss" % (argv[0], timeout)
    except OSError as e:
        return 126, "%s: %s" % (argv[0], e)
    out = ((p.stdout or "") + (p.stderr or "")).strip()
    return p.returncode, out


# --- what the health probe knows --------------------------------------------------------

_health_lock = threading.Lock()
_health_at = 0.0
_health_val = None


def probe_tmux():
    """Two booleans, each a has-session-family probe, each 2s.

    tmux_default is `has-session -t orch` on the DEFAULT socket rather than a bare
    server-reachable check, because that session is the precondition both verbs actually
    have: a default server with no `orch` session cannot receive a window, and reporting
    "yes, tmux is up" in that case would be a true statement that answers the wrong
    question.

    tmux_orch_sessions is `list-sessions` on the orch-sessions socket -- a bare
    has-session there has no target to name, so the reachability question is asked with
    the command that answers it. False means there are no detached sessions to open, not
    that anything is broken."""
    rc, _ = run([TMUX, "-L", "default", "has-session", "-t", ORCH_SESSION], PROBE_TIMEOUT)
    default_ok = rc == 0
    rc, _ = run([TMUX, "-L", ORCH_SOCKET, "list-sessions"], PROBE_TIMEOUT)
    orch_ok = rc == 0
    return default_ok, orch_ok


def health():
    global _health_at, _health_val
    now = time.monotonic()
    with _health_lock:
        if _health_val is not None and (now - _health_at) < HEALTH_TTL:
            default_ok, orch_ok = _health_val
        else:
            default_ok, orch_ok = probe_tmux()
            _health_val, _health_at = (default_ok, orch_ok), now
    try:
        mtime = datetime.fromtimestamp(PAGE.stat().st_mtime).astimezone().isoformat()
        ok = True
    except OSError:
        mtime, ok = None, False
    return {"ok": ok, "page_mtime_iso": mtime,
            "tmux_default": default_ok, "tmux_orch_sessions": orch_ok}


# --- naming a session -------------------------------------------------------------------

def session_label(uuid):
    """First 20 chars of what Claude Code called this session, else the uuid head.

    ~/.claude/sessions/*.json is keyed by pid, not by session id, so the id is a field
    inside and this is a scan rather than a lookup. Forty-odd small files; it costs
    nothing and it is the difference between a tmux window called `orch:dev-workflo` and
    one called `0696c976`."""
    root = Path.home() / ".claude" / "sessions"
    try:
        files = sorted(root.glob("*.json"))
    except OSError:
        files = []
    for p in files:
        try:
            d = json.loads(p.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            continue
        if isinstance(d, dict) and d.get("sessionId") == uuid:
            name = (d.get("name") or "").strip()
            if name:
                return name[:20]
    return uuid[:8]


def transcript_exists(uuid):
    """A session that is not running is only resumable if `claude --resume` has something
    to resume. Asking here rather than letting claude fail inside a detached tmux session
    is the difference between an error you can read and a window that dies silently."""
    root = Path.home() / ".claude" / "projects"
    try:
        return any(root.glob("*/%s.jsonl" % uuid))
    except OSError:
        return False


# --- the verbs --------------------------------------------------------------------------

class Refused(Exception):
    """The request was well-formed and the answer is no. status is what to say on the
    wire; 400 for a request that could never work, 200 for one tmux itself refused --
    that refusal is data the page should show, not an HTTP failure to swallow."""

    def __init__(self, out, status=200):
        super().__init__(out)
        self.out = out
        self.status = status


def require_window_host():
    rc, out = run([TMUX, "-L", "default", "has-session", "-t", ORCH_SESSION], PROBE_TIMEOUT)
    if rc != 0:
        raise Refused(out or "no `%s` session on the default tmux server -- "
                             "arcs-serve will not start one" % ORCH_SESSION)


def verb_open(target):
    """Put a Claude session in front of Kyle: resume it if it is not running, then open
    a window on it in the tmux he is already looking at."""
    if not UUID_RE.match(target or ""):
        raise Refused("target is not a session uuid: %r" % (target,), 400)
    steps = []

    rc, _ = run([TMUX, "-L", ORCH_SOCKET, "has-session", "-t", target], PROBE_TIMEOUT)
    if rc != 0:
        if not transcript_exists(target):
            raise Refused("no live session on %s and no transcript under "
                          "~/.claude/projects -- nothing to resume" % ORCH_SOCKET)
        rc, out = run([TMUX, "-L", ORCH_SOCKET, "new-session", "-d", "-s", target,
                       "-x", "200", "-y", "50", "claude", "--resume", target])
        if rc != 0:
            raise Refused(out or "tmux new-session failed (rc=%d)" % rc)
        steps.append("resumed %s detached on %s" % (target[:8], ORCH_SOCKET))
    else:
        steps.append("%s already live on %s" % (target[:8], ORCH_SOCKET))

    require_window_host()
    label = session_label(target)
    rc, out = run([TMUX, "-L", "default", "new-window", "-t", "%s:" % ORCH_SESSION,
                   "-n", label, TMUX, "-L", ORCH_SOCKET, "attach", "-t", target])
    if rc != 0:
        raise Refused(out or "tmux new-window failed (rc=%d)" % rc)
    steps.append("opened window %s in %s:" % (label, ORCH_SESSION))
    return "; ".join(steps) + ((" | " + out) if out else "")


def verb_opendir(target):
    """A shell in a worktree, in the same tmux. This is `orch watch`'s behaviour without
    its $TMUX requirement, which a systemd-started daemon could never satisfy."""
    raw = (target or "").strip()
    if not raw:
        raise Refused("opendir needs a directory", 400)
    p = Path(os.path.expanduser(raw))
    if not p.is_absolute():
        raise Refused("target is not an absolute path: %r" % (raw,), 400)
    try:
        real = p.resolve()
        home = Path.home().resolve()
    except OSError as e:
        raise Refused("cannot resolve %r: %s" % (raw, e), 400)
    if real != home and home not in real.parents:
        raise Refused("target is not under %s: %s" % (home, real), 400)
    if not real.is_dir():
        raise Refused("not a directory: %s" % real, 400)

    require_window_host()
    rc, out = run([TMUX, "-L", "default", "new-window", "-t", "%s:" % ORCH_SESSION,
                   "-c", str(real)])
    if rc != 0:
        raise Refused(out or "tmux new-window failed (rc=%d)" % rc)
    return "opened a shell in %s" % real


VERBS = {"open": verb_open, "opendir": verb_opendir}


# --- the terminal drawer's ttyds --------------------------------------------------------

_ttyd_lock = threading.Lock()
_ttyds = {}    # uuid -> (port, Popen)


def _free_port():
    s = socket.socket()
    try:
        s.bind(("127.0.0.1", 0))
        return s.getsockname()[1]
    finally:
        s.close()


def _reap_locked():
    for uuid in [u for u, (_, proc) in _ttyds.items() if proc.poll() is not None]:
        _ttyds.pop(uuid, None)
        log("ttyd for %s exited" % uuid[:8])


def term_url(uuid):
    """One ttyd per uuid, reaped when it dies, capped. Bound to lo by ttyd's own -i, so
    the cap and the loopback guard are both real rather than inherited from us."""
    if not UUID_RE.match(uuid or ""):
        raise Refused("target is not a session uuid: %r" % (uuid,), 400)
    with _ttyd_lock:
        _reap_locked()
        existing = _ttyds.get(uuid)
        if existing:
            return "http://127.0.0.1:%d/" % existing[0]
        if len(_ttyds) >= TERM_MAX:
            raise Refused("%d terminals already open (the cap) -- close one first"
                          % TERM_MAX)
        port = _free_port()
        argv = [TTYD, "-W", "-p", str(port), "-i", "lo",
                TMUX, "-L", ORCH_SOCKET, "attach", "-t", uuid]
        try:
            proc = subprocess.Popen(argv, stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                                    stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, env=child_env())
        except OSError as e:
            raise Refused("cannot start %s: %s" % (TTYD, e))
        _ttyds[uuid] = (port, proc)
    # Outside the lock: a 200 pointing at a port nothing is listening on yet is a broken
    # drawer, so wait for the socket rather than for a fixed sleep.
    deadline = time.monotonic() + 3.0
    while time.monotonic() < deadline:
        if proc.poll() is not None:
            with _ttyd_lock:
                _ttyds.pop(uuid, None)
            raise Refused("%s exited immediately (rc=%s)" % (TTYD, proc.returncode))
        try:
            with socket.create_connection(("127.0.0.1", port), 0.2):
                log("ttyd for %s on 127.0.0.1:%d" % (uuid[:8], port))
                return "http://127.0.0.1:%d/" % port
        except OSError:
            time.sleep(0.05)
    raise Refused("%s did not start listening on 127.0.0.1:%d" % (TTYD, port))


def kill_ttyds():
    with _ttyd_lock:
        for uuid, (_, proc) in list(_ttyds.items()):
            try:
                proc.terminate()
            except OSError:
                pass
            _ttyds.pop(uuid, None)


# --- the wire ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

class Handler(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):
    server_version = "arcs-serve"
    sys_version = ""
    protocol_version = "HTTP/1.1"

    def version_string(self):
        return self.server_version

    # --- replies ---

    def _send(self, status, body, ctype):
        if isinstance(body, str):
            body = body.encode("utf-8")
        self.send_response(status)
        self.send_header("Content-Type", ctype)
        self.send_header("Content-Length", str(len(body)))
        self.send_header("Cache-Control", "no-store")
        # Deliberately no Access-Control-* header, here or anywhere. The X-Arcs-Act guard
        # is only a guard for as long as a cross-origin preflight has nothing to succeed
        # against; one permissive CORS line would undo the whole design.
        self.end_headers()
        if self.command != "HEAD":
            self.wfile.write(body)

    def _json(self, status, obj):
        self._send(status, json.dumps(obj) + "\n", "application/json; charset=utf-8")

    def _text(self, status, msg):
        self._send(status, msg + "\n", "text/plain; charset=utf-8")

    # --- routing ---

    def do_GET(self):
        path = unquote(urlparse(self.path).path)
        try:
            if path == "/":
                return self._page()
            if path == "/health":
                return self._json(200, health())
            if path.startswith("/term/"):
                return self._term(path[len("/term/"):])
            if path == "/act":
                # Named so the mistake is legible: /act exists, and it is a POST.
                self.send_response(405)
                self.send_header("Allow", "POST")
                self.send_header("Content-Length", "0")
                self.send_header("Cache-Control", "no-store")
                self.end_headers()
                return
            return self._text(404, "no such path: %s" % path)
        except Exception as e:                        # noqa: BLE001 -- see handle_error
            log("GET %s blew up: %r" % (path, e))
            return self._text(500, "arcs-serve: %s" % e)

    def do_HEAD(self):
        self.do_GET()

    # A POST body has to leave the socket whatever the answer is. On a keep-alive
    # connection an undrained body is read as the next request line, so a 403 with the
    # payload still in the pipe answers correctly and then reports "Bad request syntax"
    # about its own leftovers -- which is how this was found.
    MAX_BODY = 1 << 20

    def _body(self):
        try:
            n = int(self.headers.get("Content-Length") or 0)
        except ValueError:
            n = 0
        if n < 0:
            n = 0
        if n > self.MAX_BODY:
            self.close_connection = True
            return None
        return self.rfile.read(n) if n else b""

    def do_POST(self):
        path = unquote(urlparse(self.path).path)
        try:
            raw = self._body()
            if raw is None:
                return self._json(413, {"ok": False, "out": "body over %d bytes"
                                                            % self.MAX_BODY})
            if path != "/act":
                return self._text(404, "no such path: %s" % path)
            return self._act(raw)
        except Exception as e:                        # noqa: BLE001
            log("POST %s blew up: %r" % (path, e))
            return self._text(500, "arcs-serve: %s" % e)

    # --- the routes themselves ---

    def _page(self):
        try:
            body = PAGE.read_bytes()
        except OSError as e:
            log("page unreadable: %s" % e)
            return self._text(503, "arcs-serve: no page at %s (%s). Run `arcs`." % (PAGE, e))
        # The charset is the entire reason this is not http.server: what arcs-page writes
        # is a fragment with no <meta charset>, and a browser guessing latin-1 turns every
        # dash and arrow on the page into mojibake.
        self._send(200, body, "text/html; charset=utf-8")

    def _term(self, rest):
        if self.headers.get(ACT_HEADER) != "1":
            return self._text(403, "arcs-serve: /term starts a process, so it needs the "
                                   "%s: 1 header" % ACT_HEADER)
        uuid = rest.strip("/")
        try:
            url = term_url(uuid)
        except Refused as r:
            log("term %s refused: %s" % (uuid[:8], r.out))
            return self._json(r.status, {"ok": False, "out": r.out})
        return self._json(200, {"ok": True, "url": url, "out": "terminal at " + url})

    def _act(self, raw):
        if self.headers.get(ACT_HEADER) != "1":
            # The body was drained by do_POST but is deliberately never parsed here: a
            # request that was not allowed to be an act should not have its payload read
            # as one.
            return self._text(403, "arcs-serve: POST /act needs the %s: 1 header"
                              % ACT_HEADER)
        try:
            req = json.loads(raw.decode("utf-8") or "{}")
        except (ValueError, UnicodeDecodeError) as e:
            return self._json(400, {"ok": False, "out": "body is not JSON: %s" % e})
        if not isinstance(req, dict):
            return self._json(400, {"ok": False, "out": "body is not a JSON object"})

        verb = req.get("verb")
        target = req.get("target")
        if verb not in VERBS:
            return self._json(400, {"ok": False,
                                    "out": "unknown verb %r -- this daemon does %s"
                                           % (verb, ", ".join(sorted(VERBS)))})
        if not isinstance(target, str):
            return self._json(400, {"ok": False, "out": "target must be a string"})

        try:
            out = VERBS[verb](target)
        except Refused as r:
            log("act %s %s refused: %s" % (verb, target, r.out))
            return self._json(r.status, {"ok": False, "out": r.out})
        log("act %s %s: %s" % (verb, target, out))
        return self._json(200, {"ok": True, "out": out})

    # --- noise control ---

    def log_message(self, fmt, *a):
        """Per-request access lines would bury the acts, which are the only lines here
        anybody will ever go looking for. Failures still speak, via log_error."""

    def log_error(self, fmt, *a):
        log("%s %s" % (self.address_string(), fmt % a))


class Server(ThreadingHTTPServer):
    daemon_threads = True
    allow_reuse_address = True

    def handle_error(self, request, client_address):
        """Loud in the log, never fatal to the serve loop. The default prints a traceback
        to stderr, which under systemd means the journal and nowhere a person looks."""
        import traceback
        log("request from %s failed: %s" % (client_address,
                                            traceback.format_exc().strip().replace("\n", " | ")))


def main():
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(description="Serve the work-arcs page locally, with verbs.")
    p.add_argument("--port", type=int,
                   default=int(os.environ.get("WORK_ARCS_SERVE_PORT") or 7379),
                   help="loopback port to listen on (default 7379, "
                        "$WORK_ARCS_SERVE_PORT)")
    args = p.parse_args()

    try:
        STATE.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    except OSError:
        pass
    trim_log()

    try:
        srv = Server(("127.0.0.1", args.port), Handler)
    except OSError as e:
        log("cannot bind 127.0.0.1:%d -- %s" % (args.port, e))
        sys.exit("arcs-serve: cannot bind 127.0.0.1:%d — %s" % (args.port, e))

    host, port = srv.server_address[0], srv.server_address[1]
    # The bound tuple, not the argument: what is printed is what the kernel gave us, so a
    # future refactor that widened the bind could not keep printing "127.0.0.1".
    log("listening on %s:%d — page %s" % (host, port, PAGE))

    def bye(_sig, _frm):
        kill_ttyds()
        log("stopping")
        # shutdown() blocks until serve_forever() returns, and a signal handler runs ON
        # the thread that is inside serve_forever() -- calling it here directly is a
        # deadlock, and the way you find out is that systemd has to SIGKILL the unit
        # every restart. So the stop is asked for from a thread that is not the one being
        # stopped.
        threading.Thread(target=srv.shutdown, daemon=True).start()

    signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, bye)
    signal.signal(signal.SIGINT, bye)
    try:
        srv.serve_forever()
    finally:
        kill_ttyds()
        trim_log()


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
